{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by the screenplay duo of jesse eisenberg and arnon milchan. the film is based on the book 'the accidental billionaire' by ben mezrich, which chronicles the founding of the social networking site facebook and the resulting lawsuits.\n\nthe film tells the story of mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, a sophomore at harvard university who creates a website called 'facemash' that becomes a huge success. this leads to the creation of facebook, initially intended as a site for harvard students only, but eventually expands to other colleges and universities.\n\nthe film explores the early days of facebook, including the site's rapid growth and the tensions that arise between zuckerberg and his co-founders, eduardo saverin, andrew mccollum, and dustin moskovitz. it also delves into the lawsuits that facebook faced, including a lawsuit filed by the winklevoss twins, who claimed that zuckerberg had stolen their idea for a social networking site.\n\njessie eisenberg's portrayal of mark zuckerberg is widely regarded as one of the standout performances of the film. his nuanced and introspective performance captures the complexities and contradictions of zuckerberg's character, and his ability to convey the intensity and focus of the young entrepreneur is impressive.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally impressive, with stand-out performances from andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, joshua jackson as dustin moskovitz, and armie hammer as the winkle-voss twin, cameron. the chemistry between the actors is palpable, and their performances add depth and nuance to the film.\n\nthe film's direction by david fincher is also noteworthy. fincher's use of a non-linear narrative structure adds to the film's tension and complexity, and his use of close-ups and quick cuts creates a sense of urgency and energy.\n\nthe film's score by trent reznor and atticus ross is equally impressive. the use of pulsating electronic beats and haunting melodies creates a sense of unease and tension, and the score perfectly complements the film's narrative.\n\nthe social network is a thought-provoking and engaging film that explores the complexities and contradictions of the tech industry. it raises important questions about the impact of technology on society, and the consequences of unchecked ambition. the film's portrayal of the cutthroat world of tech startups is both fascinating and disturbing, and it serves as a cautionary tale about the dangers of unchecked power and greed.\n\noverall, the social network is a must-see film for anyone interested in technology, business, or the social implications of technology. it is a gripping and thought-provoking film that will keep you on the edge of your seat, and it is a testament to the power of storytelling to explore complex and nuanced themes.\n\nrating: 9.5/10", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.25480825}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -2.5629668e-05}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.622601e-06}, {"token": " american", "logprob": -0.2427468}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -0.0040608337}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -0.00030286494}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.0119998}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0006139777}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.21539485}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -0.011442982}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.01051729}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -0.00034171465}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -1.6450747e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.19390231}, {"token": " written", "logprob": -0.0020268867}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -4.410734e-06